<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Illyrian Provinces</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Illyrian Provinces were a region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1809, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a>, from Austrian lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, Istria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and Ragusa. With their capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a> and governors including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-de-marmont">Auguste de Marmont</a>, they received French law and administration and schools teaching in local languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> reoccupied them in 1813–1814.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Illyrian Provinces were created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a> on 14 October 1809, after the Austrian defeat at Wagram, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> ceded western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> with Lienz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> and Gradisca, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, the March of Istria and the Croatian lands southwest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>. These territories were joined with the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-dalmatia">Venetian Dalmatia</a> and Istria and the former Republic of Ragusa to form provinces technically part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The name Illyrian was probably suggested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-de-marmont">Auguste de Marmont</a>, who wanted to support a sense of commonality among the peoples, and alluded to the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>, called Laybach, which was the seat of the governor-general. By a decree of 15 April 1811 the central government consisted of the governor-general, an intendant of finance and a commissioner of the judiciary. The governors-general were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-de-marmont">Auguste de Marmont</a>, Henri-Gatien Bertrand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-andoche-junot">Jean-Andoche Junot</a> and Joseph Fouché. Illyrian offices answered to ministries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and the inhabitants had Illyrian nationality. In 1811 the population of four intendancies, Ljubljana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlovac">Karlovac</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a>, was given as 1,504,258.</p><p>The French introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, equality before the law, compulsory military service, a uniform tax system and the separation of church and state, with civil registration of births and weddings, and they nationalised the judiciary. A decree emancipated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and abolished a Habsburg-era regulation that had forbidden Jews to settle within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. At first French, Italian and German were the official languages; Croatian and Slovenian were added in 1811, and Slovene became official for the first time. A decree of 4 July 1810 created écoles centrales at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a>, and it is now considered the charter of the University of Ljubljana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the Treaty of Tilsit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> blockaded the Adriatic, bringing merchant shipping to a standstill. In August 1813 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> again declared war. Austrian troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> on 29 October, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a> surrendered on 6 December after a 34-day siege, and the French garrisons at Kotor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> surrendered on 3 and 27 January 1814, effectively ending French rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a> of 30 May 1814 reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to its 1792 borders. The permission to use Slovene in schools won the sympathy of members of the Slovene National Awakening, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentin-vodnik">Valentin Vodnik</a> wrote school books that made Slovene teaching possible.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Illyrian Provinces” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 31 January 1808 Marshal Auguste Marmont proclaimed the abolition of the republic, and its territory was soon incorporated into the <b>Illyrian Provinces</b> of the French Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
